INSERT INTO chapters (innovation_id, position, heading, body) VALUES
  ('IND-360', 1, 'One registry for a billion arms',
'CoWIN was the health ministry''s answer to a logistics problem with no precedent: record every COVID-19 vaccine dose given in India, book appointments for well over a billion people, and hand each person proof they had been vaccinated. People registered with a mobile number and an identity document, up to four people per number, and booked a slot at a public or private centre; the vaccinator then logged each dose against that record [5].

The numbers grew fast. India passed one billion doses about nine months after its drive began, and by October 2021 the platform had issued its billionth vaccination certificate [4].'),
  ('IND-360', 2, 'A certificate you can check with a phone',
'The certificates came from DIVOC, short for Digital Infrastructure for Vaccination Open Credentialing, an open-source stack built and maintained by the Bengaluru-based eGov Foundation and released under the permissive MIT licence [3][4]. Each certificate is a W3C-style verifiable credential holding the dose details and the holder''s name, date of birth and gender. The data is sent to a central authority, signed with national keys and sent back to the centre for printing, or downloaded through a link sent by SMS [3].

Because the signature travels inside the QR code, anyone with the public key can check a certificate offline without calling a government server. DIVOC was also adopted by Sri Lanka and the Philippines, and eGov said it was working with more countries [3][4].'),
  ('IND-360', 3, 'The digital divide in the dock',
'In spring 2021 the government made online registration on CoWIN compulsory for people aged 18 to 44 and barred walk-ins for that group, citing vaccine scarcity and crowding [5]. On 31 May 2021 a Supreme Court bench led by Justice D. Y. Chandrachud said a policy relying only on a digital portal "would be unable to meet its target of universal immunization owing to such a digital divide" [5].

The court cited survey figures that only about 24% of Indian households, and about 15% of rural ones, had internet access in 2017-18, and asked the government to make CoWIN available in regional languages, audit it for disability access, and allow on-site registration at some centres [5].'),
  ('IND-360', 4, 'The 2023 leak allegation',
'On 12 June 2023, the Malayalam news portal The Fourth reported that a Telegram bot would return the name, date of birth and passport or Aadhaar number of a CoWIN registrant if fed their phone number; The News Minute tested it on several public figures and found the details accurate [6].

The health ministry called the breach reports "without any basis and mischievous in nature" and asked CERT-In, the national cyber-security agency, to investigate. The minister of state for IT, Rajeev Chandrasekhar, said the bot appeared to draw on a database of previously stolen data rather than a direct breach of CoWIN [6]. Where that earlier data came from was not publicly settled, which is why the caveat on this entry remains.');
